WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.330
 I have a saying that
your food is your flag.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.540
Food can tell us where we
come from and who we are.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.330
I'm a culinary historian,
historic interpreter,

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:13.710
and the James
Beard Award-winning

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.330
author of "The Cooking Gene."

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.190
The last thing
people will give up

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.010
as they make journeys and
migrations is their food

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.540
culture.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.270
You have to look at
one particular dish,

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.240
and then you can trace it
back to its origin story;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.240
and you ask the question, "why
did my family make that food?"

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.270
You begin to get a
picture of how you're

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.695
situated in a larger story.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.520
This class is not a
full-on cooking school

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.630
of African-American
culinary technique.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:48.400
It's about new ways to express
our relationship to food

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.440
through writing,
through storytelling,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.850
through genealogy.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.910
What you will learn, is how to
document your own food story

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.670
and have a greater context
for where we come from.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:01.840
Go to the eldest people who are
able to tell you the stories

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.100
and answer your questions.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.530
You're going to have a moment
where you close your eyes

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.110
and you open them,
and that entire table

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.060
will be surrounded
by the people that

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.440
populated that person's life.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.060
You have to be willing to go
into the positive and negative

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.880
aspects of your family
history and journey

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.920
if you really want
to do this work.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.200
We have to talk about
traumatic stuff--

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.490
history, the concept of
race, that potato famine,

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.090
that Holocaust,
that slave trade.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.510
That's only part of the
puzzle, but in confronting

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.820
the issues that
come off of that,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.890
we become better,
because we understand

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.620
what it took to get us here.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.230
What I'm trying to offer you
is a little bit of insight

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.480
you can do your own
journeying through food

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.450
to find the ancestral
wisdom in the kitchen that's

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.470
right for you.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.897
And if I can do it, I hope
that you will as well;

00:01:55.897 --> 00:01:57.480
I hope that you will
have the strength

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.170
to say that your story
matters as much as mine does.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.240
I'm Michael Twitty, and
this is MasterClass.

